Title = {Molecular and functional profiling of Gαi as an intracellular pH sensor},
Journal = {NATURE COMMUNICATIONS},
Year = {2025},
Volume = {16},
Number = {1},
Month = {APR 11},
Abstract = {Heterotrimeric G proteins (G alpha, G beta and G gamma) act downstream
   of G-protein-coupled receptors (GPCRs) to mediate signaling pathways
   that regulate various physiological processes and human disease
   conditions. While human G alpha i and its yeast homolog Gpa1 were
   previously postulated to function as intracellular pH sensors, the
   pH-sensing capabilities of G alpha i and the underlying mechanism remain
   to be established. Our research shows that variations in pH
   significantly affect the structure and stability of G alpha i-GDP.
   Specifically, at the lower end of the physiological pH range, the
   protein undergoes an order-to-disorder transition due to the loss of
   electrostatic interactions within the G alpha i Switch regions,
   resulting in a reduction in agonist-mediated G alpha i-G beta gamma
   release. Further, we identified key residues within the G alpha i Switch
   regions that form the pH-sensing network. Mutation of these residues in
   G alpha i gives rise to `low pH mimetics' that abolish pH-dependent
   thermostability changes and reduce G alpha i-G beta gamma release.
   Overall, our findings suggest that pH-sensitive structural changes in G
   alpha i impact the agonist-mediated dissociation of G beta gamma, which
   is essential for proper signaling.},
